UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52948288247321
DTSTART:20261028T123000Z
DTEND:20261028T230000Z
DESCRIPTION:Join Family Enterprise Center faculty and your family enterpris
 e peers for an immersive two-day conference. The Forum will be a hands-on 
 experience to help you:\n\nStep away from your daily responsibilities to f
 ocus on the future of your family enterprise.\nExplore multiple aspects of
  your family enterprise: ownership\, family\, business\, and self – to d
 etermine what is working well and where you may need to make changes in th
 e future.\nLearn from business school faculty with the goal of applying cu
 rrent research and thought leadership to your own family enterprise. Build
  relationships with other families.\nLeave with tangible next steps and an
  accountability plan. The Forum will be helpful for owners of family enter
 prises at every stage of growth. Participants will find it useful to bring
  multiple family members from more than one generation. Non-family executi
 ves will also benefit from this program. If you are a current student and 
 interested in attending the Family Business Forum with your family through
  a fellowship\, please reach out to Gwen Cummings at Gwen_Cummings@kenan-f
 lagler.unc.edu for more information.
GEO:35.912124;-79.006016
LOCATION:Rizzo Conference Center \, 340
SUMMARY:The Family Enterprise Center's 13th Annual Family Business Forum 
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/the-family-enterprise-centers-
 13th-annual-family-business-forum
CATEGORIES:Meetings and Conferences
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260611T043851Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52242577665852
DTSTART:20261028T130000Z
DTEND:20261028T190000Z
DESCRIPTION:This course features Dynamic Narrative Inquiry – theory and p
 rinciples for narrative research design and analysis drawing on the richne
 ss of expressive language. With this approach to qualitative inquiry\, res
 earchers can build on communication in daily life where diverse practices 
 for sharing experience\, making sense of experience\, and imagining social
  change occur in personal narratives\, sociocultural communications\, and 
 institutional policies.\n\nThis course involves a sequence of presentation
 s and practical workshops with narrative analysis strategies applicable to
  small or large studies that sample verbal and/or visual discourse. We beg
 in with an introduction to dynamic narrative inquiry\, followed by modules
  with three analysis strategies that yield findings to a wide range of rel
 evant social science research questions. With examples from prior publishe
 d studies\, I present rationales and methodology\, including character map
 ping (to identify meaning in narrative actors\, actions\, and relationship
 s)\; plot analysis (to identify how narrative structures express logics an
 d intentions)\; and values analysis (to identify speaker/author/ purposes)
 . The final hour of the day is devoted to discussing course participants
 ’ insights from the workshops and implications for future research\, pra
 ctice\, and policy.\n\nRegistration and fee (refundable for students) are 
 required for Zoom link.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Narrative Analysis for the Qualitative Researcher (online)
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/narrative-analysis-for-the-qua
 litative-researcher-online
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
